                {"title": "Are You Experienced", "year": 1967, "creator": "The Jimi Hendrix Experience",
                 "origin": "Track · UK", "length": "40 min", "hours": 0.67,
                 "why": "Jimi Hendrix was an unknown American backing guitarist when Chas Chandler of the Animals brought him to London in 1966 and built a trio around him. On this debut he uses feedback, fuzz and the whammy bar as parts of the songs rather than as effects, most clearly on 'Third Stone from the Sun' and the backwards guitar of the title track. A month after it came out in Britain he set his guitar on fire at the Monterey Pop Festival in California.",
                 "start": "Choose an edition with 'Purple Haze', 'Hey Joe' and 'The Wind Cries Mary'. The original UK album left those singles off, the American one swapped them in, and most modern reissues include everything.",
                 "extra": {"genre": "Rock"}},
                {"title": "At Folsom Prison", "year": 1968, "creator": "Johnny Cash",
                 "origin": "Columbia · USA", "length": "45 min", "hours": 0.75,
                 "why": "Johnny Cash had asked Columbia for years to let him record a prison show, and on January 13, 1968, he played two concerts at Folsom State Prison in California with June Carter, Carl Perkins and the Statler Brothers. The set leans on songs about crime and jail, from 'Cocaine Blues' to '25 Minutes to Go'. He closes with 'Greystone Chapel', written by a Folsom inmate, Glen Sherley, which Cash had learned the night before. The album revived a career slowed by drug problems.",
                 "start": "Hear the original LP running order first. The 2008 Legacy Edition has both full concerts if you want to hear how the shows actually ran, between-song talk included.",
                 "extra": {"genre": "Country"}},
                {"title": "Os Mutantes", "year": 1968, "creator": "Os Mutantes",
                 "origin": "Polydor · Brazil", "length": "36 min", "hours": 0.6,
                 "why": "The debut by a São Paulo trio, brothers Arnaldo Baptista and Sérgio Dias with singer Rita Lee, part of the Tropicália movement that mixed Brazilian music with psychedelic rock while the country lived under a military government. They cover songs by Gilberto Gil, Caetano Veloso and Jorge Ben, sing one song in French, and pile on fuzz guitar and tape effects. Their older brother Cláudio built much of their equipment by hand.",
                 "start": "Start with 'Panis et Circenses' and 'A Minha Menina'; you do not need Portuguese. Then try the group album Tropicália: ou Panis et Circencis (1968), where they play alongside Gil and Veloso.",
                 "extra": {"genre": "Tropicália"}},
                {"title": "Astral Weeks", "year": 1968, "creator": "Van Morrison",
                 "origin": "Warner Bros. · Northern Ireland", "length": "47 min", "hours": 0.78,
                 "why": "Van Morrison, a 23-year-old Belfast singer best known for 'Brown Eyed Girl', recorded these eight songs in New York in three sessions with jazz players, including bassist Richard Davis and the Modern Jazz Quartet's drummer, Connie Kay. The musicians followed his voice and guitar with little rehearsal, which is why the songs drift and circle instead of building to choruses. It sold poorly on release, and Lester Bangs wrote one of rock criticism's best-known essays about it in 1979.",
                 "start": "Play it start to finish, at night if you can. Strings were added afterward, but the core is bass, acoustic guitar and voice. 'Madame George' and 'Cyprus Avenue' are the heart of it.",
                 "extra": {"genre": "Folk rock"}},
                {"title": "Dusty in Memphis", "year": 1969, "creator": "Dusty Springfield",
                 "origin": "Atlantic · UK", "length": "33 min", "hours": 0.54,
                 "why": "Dusty Springfield, the biggest British female pop star of the 1960s, signed with Atlantic and recorded at American Sound Studio in Memphis with producers Jerry Wexler, Tom Dowd and Arif Mardin. Unhappy with how she sounded there, she sang her final vocals later in New York. The songs come from Burt Bacharach, Randy Newman and Goffin and King, and 'Son of a Preacher Man' was first offered to Aretha Franklin, who turned it down and later recorded it herself.",
                 "start": "Start with 'Son of a Preacher Man', then 'Breakfast in Bed'. The original eleven songs run just over half an hour, so play them in order.",
                 "extra": {"genre": "Soul and pop"}},
                {"title": "Abbey Road", "year": 1969, "creator": "The Beatles",
                 "origin": "Apple · UK", "length": "47 min", "hours": 0.79,
                 "why": "The last album the four Beatles recorded together, made in the summer of 1969 after the tense sessions that later became Let It Be. Side one has George Harrison's 'Something' and John Lennon's nearly eight-minute 'I Want You (She's So Heavy)', which stops dead mid-riff. Side two opens with Harrison's 'Here Comes the Sun' and ends with a sixteen-minute medley of song fragments stitched together by Paul McCartney and George Martin. The cover photo on the zebra crossing took about ten minutes.",
                 "start": "Hear side two in one go, from 'Here Comes the Sun' to 'The End', and stay for the short hidden 'Her Majesty'. Giles Martin's 2019 remix is a good modern option.",
                 "extra": {"genre": "Rock"}},
                {"title": "Bitches Brew", "year": 1970, "creator": "Miles Davis",
                 "origin": "Columbia · USA", "length": "1 hr 34 min", "hours": 1.57,
                 "why": "Miles Davis put two drummers, several electric keyboard players, bass clarinet and John McLaughlin's guitar in a New York studio for three days in August 1969 and let them play long, loose grooves. Producer Teo Macero then cut, spliced and looped the tapes into the finished pieces, so parts of the title track repeat sections that were played only once. It was a double album with tracks up to 27 minutes long, and it became Davis's first gold record.",
                 "start": "Start with 'Miles Runs the Voodoo Down' (about 14 minutes) before the long title track. If this is your first electric Miles, In a Silent Way (1969) is a shorter, calmer way in.",
                 "extra": {"genre": "Jazz fusion"}},
                {"title": "Paranoid", "year": 1970, "creator": "Black Sabbath",
                 "origin": "Vertigo · UK", "length": "42 min", "hours": 0.7,
                 "why": "Four musicians from industrial Birmingham recorded their second album in a few days in 1970 and set the template for heavy metal: Tony Iommi's slow, crushing riffs, Ozzy Osbourne wailing over them, and songs about war, drugs and nuclear dread. The title track was written in the studio as filler when the album came up short. The record was meant to be called War Pigs, after the opener about generals sending the poor to die, and the cover's sword-carrying figure is left over from that title.",
                 "start": "Side one, from 'War Pigs' through 'Planet Caravan' to 'Iron Man', is the core of it. Master of Reality (1971), the next album, is where Iommi tuned his guitar down and the sound got heavier still.",
                 "extra": {"genre": "Metal"}},
                {"title": "Journey in Satchidananda", "year": 1971, "creator": "Alice Coltrane",
                 "origin": "Impulse! · USA", "length": "37 min", "hours": 0.62,
                 "why": "Alice Coltrane, a pianist and harpist who had played in John Coltrane's last band, recorded this album in 1970, three years after his death, while studying with the Indian teacher Swami Satchidananda. The title track sets her harp glissandos over Cecil McBee's repeating bass line, a tamboura drone and Pharoah Sanders's soprano saxophone. 'Isis and Osiris' was recorded live at the Village Gate in New York and adds an oud and Charlie Haden on bass.",
                 "start": "Start with the title track and let it set the mood for the rest. Next, try Pharoah Sanders's Karma (1969), whose 32-minute 'The Creator Has a Master Plan' comes from the same circle.",
                 "extra": {"genre": "Spiritual jazz"}},
                {"title": "What's Going On", "year": 1971, "creator": "Marvin Gaye",
                 "origin": "Tamla · USA", "length": "35 min", "hours": 0.59,
                 "starter": True,
                 "why": "Marvin Gaye wrote and produced this song cycle about the Vietnam War, poverty, police violence and pollution, partly from conversations with his brother Frankie, who had served in Vietnam. Motown boss Berry Gordy did not want to release the title song and gave in only after Gaye refused to record anything else. The tracks run into one another without breaks, and the saxophone line that opens the album came from Eli Fontaine warming up. Rolling Stone's 2020 list ranked it the greatest album of all time.",
                 "start": "Play all nine songs in order; it works as one continuous piece. The 2001 Deluxe Edition adds the earlier Detroit mix, which is worth hearing once you know the album well.",
                 "extra": {"genre": "Soul"}},
                {"title": "Blue", "year": 1971, "creator": "Joni Mitchell",
                 "origin": "Reprise · Canada", "length": "36 min", "hours": 0.6,
                 "starter": True,
                 "why": "Joni Mitchell wrote most of these ten songs after her breakup with Graham Nash and a long trip through Europe, including a stay among the hippies living in the caves at Matala on Crete, which turns up in 'Carey'. She plays guitar, piano and Appalachian dulcimer, with sparse help from James Taylor and a few others. The lyrics were unusually direct for 1971, and Rolling Stone's 2020 list put it at number three, the highest place for an album by a woman.",
                 "start": "'River', which opens with a sad piano take on 'Jingle Bells', and 'A Case of You' are the most covered songs here. Court and Spark (1974) is the next Mitchell album to hear.",
                 "extra": {"genre": "Folk"}},
                {"title": "Maggot Brain", "year": 1971, "creator": "Funkadelic",
                 "origin": "Westbound · USA", "length": "37 min", "hours": 0.62,
                 "why": "George Clinton's Detroit-based band mixed funk, gospel and loud psychedelic rock, and the title track is its peak: a ten-minute guitar solo by Eddie Hazel over a slow, faint backing. Clinton has said he told Hazel to play as if he had just been told his mother had died, then learned it was not true. Clinton then mixed most of the band almost out of the track. The other six songs, from 'Can You Get to That' to the noisy 'Wars of Armageddon', show the funk side.",
                 "start": "Start with the title track, ideally on headphones, then play the rest. Parliament's Mothership Connection (1975) is the next stop for Clinton's other band.",
                 "extra": {"genre": "Funk"}},
                {"title": "Led Zeppelin IV", "year": 1971, "creator": "Led Zeppelin",
                 "origin": "Atlantic · UK", "length": "42 min", "hours": 0.71,
                 "aka": ["Untitled", "Four Symbols", "Zoso"],
                 "why": "The fourth Led Zeppelin album has no title or band name on its cover, only an old painting of a man carrying a bundle of sticks, and four symbols chosen by the members. Much of it was recorded at Headley Grange, a run-down house in Hampshire, using the Rolling Stones' mobile studio. For 'When the Levee Breaks', John Bonham's drums were set up in the hall and miked from the stairwell above, which gives them their huge echo. It also has 'Stairway to Heaven' and Sandy Denny on 'The Battle of Evermore'.",
                 "start": "'Black Dog' opens it and 'When the Levee Breaks' closes it. Members may have saved it as Untitled or by its symbols. Led Zeppelin II (1969) is the harder, bluesier record to try next.",
                 "extra": {"genre": "Rock"}},
